  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improving Menstrual Hygiene Management for Rohingya Women and Girls in Cox&#x2019;s Bazar</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since the onset of the Rohingya refugee emergency humanitarian response in Cox&#x2019;s Bazar, Bangladesh in 2017, various humanitarian actors have been engaged in a range of activities aimed at improving the dignity, hygiene practices and menstrual hygiene management of the Rohingya women and girls. While the initiatives have made considerable gains over the course of the Rohingya response, the need for creating a sustainable system for the continuous delivery of safe menstruation management for the Rohingya women and girls is growing with gradually decreased humanitarian funding stream. The fast fashion industries in Bangladesh produce a large volume of fabric waste. The project aims to engage with businesses within the fashion industry to promote circular supply chains and encourage the re-use or repurposing of textiles for as long as possible. Making it possible to lessen the environmental impact of manufacturing these fabrics and reducing the volume of waste. The project will create a linkage with private sector for a sustainable pad production to improve the menstrual hygiene management of the Rohingya women and girls.</narrative>
  </description>
